       The propagation type assigned to a new mount depends on the propagation
       type of the parent mount.  If the mount has a parent  (i.e.,  it  is  a
       non‐root  mount)  and  the propagation type of the parent is MMSS__SSHHAARREEDD,
       then the propagation type of the new mount is also  MMSS__SSHHAARREEDD.   Other‐
       wise, the propagation type of the new mount is MMSS__PPRRIIVVAATTEE.

       Notwithstanding  the  fact  that  the  default propagation type for new
       mount is in many cases MMSS__PPRRIIVVAATTEE, MMSS__SSHHAARREEDD is typically more  useful.
       For  this  reason,  ssyysstteemmdd(1)  automatically  remounts  all  mounts as
       MMSS__SSHHAARREEDD on system startup.  Thus, on most modern systems, the default
       propagation type is in practice MMSS__SSHHAARREEDD.

       Since, when one uses uunnsshhaarree(1) to create a mount namespace,  the  goal
       is  commonly  to  provide full isolation of the mounts in the new name‐
       space, uunnsshhaarree(1) (since _u_t_i_l_-_l_i_n_u_x 2.27) in  turn  reverses  the  step
       performed  by ssyysstteemmdd(1), by making all mounts private in the new name‐
       space.  That is, uunnsshhaarree(1) performs the equivalent of the following in
       the new mount namespace:

           mount --make-rprivate /

       To prevent this, one can use the _-_-_p_r_o_p_a_g_a_t_i_o_n _u_n_c_h_a_n_g_e_d option to  uunn‐‐
       sshhaarree(1).

       An  application  that  creates  a  new  mount  namespace directly using
       cclloonnee(2) or uunnsshhaarree(2) may  desire  to  prevent  propagation  of  mount
       events  to other mount namespaces (as is done by uunnsshhaarree(1)).  This can
       be done by changing the propagation type of mounts in the new namespace
       to either MMSS__SSLLAAVVEE or MMSS__PPRRIIVVAATTEE, using a call such as the following:

           mount(NULL, "/", MS_SLAVE | MS_REC, NULL);

